## Package comparison

The Phase 3 package remains aligned with the approved plan, and this round changes only the allowed focused test. The two-client graph now has strong exact connection/forwarder/provider finalization counts. Remaining package-owned process and lifecycle cases still prevent approval.

## Findings

### High — Executable cleanup failure remains unverified at the process boundary

Signal-handler removal and direct Layer composition are covered, but no runtime executable-path test proves signal-driven dependency-order cleanup or that close/unlink failure sets nonzero process status while retaining tagged operation/message diagnostics. Promise-facade and direct-Layer failure tests do not establish the Node process boundary required by the package.

### Medium — Blocked-sampling exact cleanup still omits close/unlink and source-acquisition counts

The blocked-sampling test covers a real socket, listener finalization, input queue/processor, connection, sample interruption, event-source finalization, and provider-Layer finalization. It still does not count listener close/unlink attempts or provider source acquisition, leaving exact-once ownership partly inferred from path absence.

### Medium — Production closing-state refusal remains unobserved

Starting `server.close()` at Node callback entry exercises the enrolled-and-finalized branch because the synchronous callback continues before the Effect runtime sets `closing`. Synthetic `canEnroll` refusal proves generic rejection, but no callback deterministically observes the real production `closing` branch.

### Medium — Post-hello late-forwarder isolation remains conflated with provider teardown

The replay-forwarding test emits its late event only after the entire provider source is disposed. The peer-isolation test proves two forwarders finalize and the healthy peer receives a later update, but it does not count write attempts attributable to the disconnected connection. Add deterministic no-late-write evidence while provider, listener, and healthy peer remain live.

### Medium — The strengthened two-client test itself is not failure-safe

Despite now owning the most comprehensive graph, the test has no `try/finally`; any assertion before its final `dispose()`/`close()` leaks both clients, the listener, and socket path. Several older integration tests have the same issue. The package explicitly requires cleanup even when assertions fail.

### Medium — Complete graph cleanup does not count close/unlink attempts

The new graph test counts listener finalization and checks path absence, but does not instrument `onClose` and `onUnlink`. Since the acceptance claim is exact-once complete graph cleanup, include those counters alongside the other exact lifecycle counts rather than inferring them from final state.

